{
  "id": "ui-slide-over",
  "title": "Slide-over: an overlay panel that is a real width on desktop and full-bleed on a phone",
  "description": "A slide-over is an ARRANGEMENT, and this recipe is only the arrangement — `logic-toggle-details-panel` owns the wiring that flips it (a Switch, `onClick` -> `flip`, `state` -> the panel's `open`), and the two are meant to be read together rather than merged. A real overlay IS authorable on this canvas, and this is how. (1) THE OVERLAY LAYER is a Group with `position: \"fixed\"`, width and height 100%, laid out `row` with `justifyContent: \"flex-end\"`. Measured at 1280x900 it is 1280x900 — the viewport — while the page canvas that contains it is only 1280x278. `\"absolute\"` also overlays, but its box is the nearest positioned ancestor (every Group is `position: relative` by default), so on a short page an absolute slide-over is a short slide-over; `\"fixed\"` is the one that does not depend on how tall the page happens to be. (2) THE SCRIM is a second box, `position: \"absolute\"` at 100%x100%, painted `var(--foreground)` with `opacity` 0.5 — no colour token carries an alpha channel, so `opacity` is how a scrim gets its tint. It is the FIRST child, because child order is paint order, and it carries the click that closes. (3) ONE WIDTH, NOT TWO: the panel is `width: 100%` with `maxWidth: 420px`. Measured, that is 420px at 1280 and 390px full-bleed at 390 with no breakpoint anywhere — there are no media queries on this canvas, and a panel pinned at 420px is 30px wider than a phone. Full-bleed also means no scrim is left to tap, which is why the header's close button is not optional. (4) THE BODY carries `scrollEnabled: true` and `height: 100%`, so a long panel scrolls inside itself: measured in a 1280x300 window the body reports scrollHeight 284 against clientHeight 231 while the page height stays 300 — the panel scrolled and the page did not grow. (5) STATE WITH NO LOGIC NODE: `open` goes straight to the overlay's `mounted`. ⚠️ Use `mounted`, not `visible` — `visible: false` only sets `visibility: hidden`, so the panel keeps its box and its whole subtree (measured: 30 boxes closed with `visible`, 16 with `mounted`, against 30 open). ⚠️ Both ports on the Component Inputs node are plugged \"output\", because a component INPUT is a port whose own plug is \"output\"; plugged \"input\" it becomes an output, and every connection drawn out of it is dropped as unhealthy.",
  "demonstrates": [
    "Group",
    "Text",
    "net.noodl.controls.button",
    "Component Inputs",
    "Component Outputs"
  ],
